nodecode-prompt-optimizer is a rewrite of Sahil-SS9's Sahil-SS9/hermes-multichannel-prompt-optimizer:

  https://github.com/Sahil-SS9/hermes-multichannel-prompt-optimizer
  commit e83eba9a8e102ed255a5bfd20cc88ea50e3af28f

MIT, Copyright (c) 2026 Sahil Saghir (LICENSE)

What was rewritten: the idea, the five-dimension scorer (clarity, specificity,
structure, actionability, plus a token estimate), the family-and-capability
tailoring of the rewrite instruction, and the operator-facing page of before and
after numbers. The Python engine, its tests and its Hermes hooks were read and
reimplemented in Common Lisp against Nodecode's cell contract: the rewrite is
one side call on the auxiliary model, the history is a JSONL file under the
home, and the surfaces are a slash command, a tool and a manual topic.

What was left out, and why: the pre-message hook that rewrote every prompt of
five words or more before the agent saw it, with its interactive approval
overlay, because Nodecode's cell contract asks for a rewrite the operator or the
model chooses rather than one that silently edits the conversation; the SQLite
metrics database, daily pruning and HTML reports, because one JSONL line a
rewrite answers what /prompt-stats needs without a schema; langid language
detection and the Unicode-range fallback, because the rewriting instruction
keeps the prompt's language without a dependency; and the twenty-one vendor
families of model-profiles.yaml, of which five are kept, for the tactics a
Nodecode user is likely to reach for.
